    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Write nine different positive integers, one in each circle in the diagram, such that the product of the three numbers along any line is the same and also the least possible. What is the least possible value of the product?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2018-g0708/q29.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "24"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "36"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "48"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "60"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "72"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Pairs with equal product",
        "body": "Each of the four straight lines passes through the centre $c$ and two opposite outer circles. So every opposite pair must have the same product $k$, and the line product is $c \\cdot k$. We need 4 different pairs of different numbers with product $k$, none of them equal to $c$.\n\n- $c = 1$: we need $k$ with 4 factor pairs not using 1, i.e. at least 10 divisors. The smallest such number is 48: $2\\cdot24, 3\\cdot16, 4\\cdot12, 6\\cdot8$. Product $1 \\cdot 48 = 48$.\n- $c = 2$: $k$ needs 8 divisors, so $k \\ge 24$, and $k = 24$ uses $2 \\cdot 12$, leaving only 3 pairs; so $k > 24$ and the product is more than $2 \\cdot 24 = 48$.\n- $c \\ge 3$: a product below 48 means $k \\le 15$, and no number up to 15 has 8 divisors.\n\nSo the least product is 48.\n\n**Answer: (C) 48**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
